---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/guides/consent';
const title = 'SMS consent collection on Shopify';
const desc = 'Design a traceable path from the wording a customer saw to the permission checked at send time. A phone number on an order is not an SMS marketing permission record.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">SMS operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-12">

<h2>Separate contact data from permission</h2>
<p><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/customer-contact-information">Shopify documents separate marketing collection settings</a> and explains that checkout disclosures should reflect abandoned-cart reminders when those are enabled. Review the actual storefront, checkout and capture apps rather than assuming a customer profile proves how the number entered the system.</p>
<p>Inventory every source: checkout, popup, keyword, customer-service entry, event form and migration. For each, identify the brand, country, message purpose, disclosure version and action that records agreement. Maintain separate email and SMS preferences. A purchase, shipping phone number or email subscription should not silently enroll a customer into promotional texts.</p>
<h2>Define the record you need to reconstruct</h2>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Operator question it answers</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Normalized number and customer/source identifier</td><td>Which contact and capture event does this permission concern?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Timestamp, source URL/form and disclosure version</td><td>When, where and under what wording did the customer act?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Brand, channel, purpose and current status</td><td>Does the proposed campaign match what was requested?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Confirmation status where used or required</td><td>Was the intended confirmation completed?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Revocation time, source and propagation status</td><td>Which queued messages were cancelled and which systems received the update?</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Store the historical disclosure separately from an editable live form. A screenshot of today's popup cannot establish last year's wording. Limit access and retention according to your privacy and legal requirements; collecting extra personal information is not automatically better evidence.</p>
<h2>Review the complete capture experience</h2>
<p>Ask the responsible legal reviewer to approve wording for the actual markets and messaging technology. Include sender identity, proposed marketing purpose, expected frequency language, applicable terms and privacy links, and the unsubscribe process. Do not treat a generic US template or an app's default checkbox as worldwide legal clearance.</p>
<p>Read the incentive and the disclosure together. If a popup promises a single stock notification, a continuing promotional program changes the expectation. If you want broader marketing permission, collect it clearly instead of expanding a narrow request inside the automation. Keep form versions tied to campaigns so a redesign does not erase the evidence behind existing subscribers.</p>
<h2>Migration: import evidence before send eligibility</h2>
<p>Export consent and suppression histories from the old provider before disconnecting it. Reconcile numbers, duplicate profiles, timestamps and negative preferences. Keep records with missing provenance out of promotional audiences while you resolve the source; importing a number should not create a new opt-in time.</p>
<p>Where two tools disagree, investigate the event sequence instead of letting the latest file upload reset an unsubscribe. Document a deliberate resubscription separately from an administrative edit. Do not send an unsolicited promotional text merely to ask whether an unverified number wants marketing.</p>
<h2>Revocation must reach the queue</h2>
<p><a class="link" href="https://www.twilio.com/en-us/legal/messaging-policy">Twilio's Messaging Policy</a> requires consent and an opt-out mechanism for messages using its services. That supplier policy is one layer of review; it does not establish the complete legal rules for every Shopify app or country. Choose a sender that exposes unsubscribe events and explain who handles free-text requests the keyword processor does not classify.</p>
<p>Cancel pending marketing when a revocation arrives, update the authoritative preference record, and propagate it to integrations. Recheck eligibility immediately before sending. Keep service communication purposes distinct; do not use a transactional sender to bypass a marketing opt-out. Escalate ambiguous requests to an owner rather than leaving them in an unattended inbox.</p>
<h2>Proposed acceptance cases before activation</h2>
<p>Use controlled records to check: a shipping-only phone number stays out; an email-only opt-in stays out; a completed SMS capture retains its disclosure version; an opt-out cancels a queued reminder; a subsequent import preserves suppression; and a deliberate resubscription has its own evidence. These are recommended checks, not results from testing the apps on this site.</p>
<p>Repeat the checks after changing forms, checkout configuration, provider or sync logic. Record the observed event times and queue outcomes. A successful STOP reply on one sender does not prove that a second sender or an offline CSV audience received the suppression.</p>
<h2>Choose tools by the audit trail they expose</h2>
<p>Request sample consent exports, revocation webhooks, retry behavior and queue cancellation logs from each candidate. Ask who owns the permission record when two platforms send for the same store. Evaluate those artifacts before comparing capture conversion rates.</p>
<p><a class="link" href="https://www.sequenzy.com/pricing">Sequenzy lists an SMS/MMS beta</a> alongside email on paid plans, with consent and STOP controls advertised. Confirm export detail, destination support and suppression propagation for your setup; an advertised feature is not a completed integration check. Apply the same demonstration requirements to every provider.</p>
<p>The output of this guide is a source inventory, versioned disclosure archive, permission schema and revocation ownership map. Use <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet hours</a> for scheduling policy and <a class="link" href="/guides/deliverability">deliverability</a> for transport failures. Neither a delivery receipt nor a successful checkout replaces the consent record.</p>

    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
